DXMT: count the frame latency limit to presentation when vsync or a frame cap holds the game; follow
DXGI's rules for SetMaximumFrameLatency; add dxgi.maxFrameLatency.

This repository's change, written by an AI agent for mavrik (S215). DXMT does not accept code written
or co-written by AI (its CONTRIBUTING.md and AGENTS.md, 2026-07-27), so it is not offered as a pull
request; the finding and the numbers go to DXMT as an issue. Against DXMT 3b252590cb (first made
against a148782d43; only the two call-table hunks moved, by 18 lines).

DXMT's frame latency limit (3 frames by default) counts a frame as done when its GPU work finishes.
Under vsync or a frame cap, finished frames then wait in Core Animation's three drawables for their
turn on screen, and that wait was not counted: mavrik's S214 meter measured about 2.4 frames of extra
input latency on a light vsync-held game (H3 5.87 frames against G3 3.36, H1 3.89 against G1 1.52).
DXVK and Windows count a frame against the limit until it is presented.

- winemetal: MetalDrawable_signalEventWhenPresented (unix call 146, in both call tables) registers a
  presented handler that sets a shared event to the frame's number. The handler runs on a Core
  Animation thread without Wine's thread state, so it only touches Metal, and never lowers the value.
- dxmt: a present carries its frame and whether it waits for presentation (SyncInterval above 0 or
  d3d11.preferredMaxFrameRate set). The encode thread registers the handler before presenting and
  records per frame whether it did. After waiting for frame N - L's GPU work, PresentBoundary also
  waits for its presentation when that frame registered a handler. Vsync off is unchanged: no handler,
  no wait. A wait that passes 100 ms is given up, logged once, and presentation waits stop until a
  frame is presented again, so a hidden or closing window never holds the game.
- d3d11: SetMaximumFrameLatency(0) resets to 3 and values above 16 return E_INVALIDARG (Microsoft's
  documentation); GetMaximumFrameLatency returns the application's value. dxgi.maxFrameLatency (DXVK's
  name and meaning, 0 to 16, default 0) caps the latency in use; 0 changes nothing.

Not here: releasing the waitable swapchain's object at presentation (the plan's step 2, it needs a
helper thread) and a latency sleep (step 4).
.factory/design-passes/2026-10-27-s214/dxmt-latency-plan.md in the mavrik repository has the analysis.

